{0 Rocq Proof Scope}

Kairos uses two distinct reference points:

- the active OCaml pipeline defines the architecture and the objects actually
  consumed by the implementation;
- the frozen POPL PaperCore development defines the Rocq theorem against which
  the corresponding mathematical claims are compared.

The Rocq proof decomposition is not an implementation architecture.  In
particular, its historical Stage 1 / Stage 2 split must not cause Kairos to
retain duplicate OCaml records or unused projection modules.

{1 Frozen POPL Reference}

The machine-readable source is [docs/rocq_alignment_manifest.json]:

{[
repository /Users/fdabrowski/Repos/kairos/kairos-spec/kairos-rocq
branch     development
commit     f3facc051901245e33a4d79676fcff6fcd464087
]}

The commit is immutable.  Moving a worktree or advancing another paper branch
does not change the reference until the manifest is deliberately updated.

The public results tracked by Kairos are:

- [local_obligations_sound];
- [generated_obligations_sound];
- [concrete_environment_generated_obligations_sound];
- [true_annotations_have_semantic_witness].

They establish the POPL proof principle for a transition system equipped with
supplied assumption and guarantee automata, product-local certificates and
generated obligations.

{1 Active Implementation Boundary}

The implementation path is read directly from the OCaml pipeline:

{[
Verification_model
  + supplied automata
  -> From_model / Product_build
  -> Ir.product_step_summary
  -> Pre
  -> Product_reachability
  -> Post
  -> Temporal_lower
  -> Step_contract_projection
  -> Proof_plan
  -> Why3 compilation and proof execution
]}

[Ir.product_step_summary] is the active carrier for product transition
identity, requirements, safe cases, unsafe cases and postconditions.
[Step_contract_projection] constructs the contracts actually consumed by
[Proof_plan].  [Proof_plan] makes prover-independent grouping and sharing
decisions.  Why3 only translates that plan.

There is no additional OCaml [KernelClause], [TimedFact], clause-family or
product-summary projection layer.  Such records were unused copies introduced
only to mirror the internal organization of an older Rocq development.

{1 What Rocq Covers}

The POPL development supplies the mathematical account for:

- transition-system execution and source transition priority;
- assumption and guarantee automata supplied as parameters;
- product states and product transitions;
- local invariant obligations;
- generated-obligation soundness;
- global assume-guarantee validity;
- the historical-expression frontier represented at paper level.

{1 What Remains Outside}

The frozen POPL theorem does not certify:

- parsing, typing, desugaring or the complete Kairos frontend;
- Spot translation from LTL to automata;
- the OCaml product-exploration algorithm;
- the exact OCaml construction of step contracts;
- [Proof_plan] grouping, factorization or sharing;
- Why3 encoding, solver scheduling, Z3, diagnostics or dumps.

These exclusions do not make those implementation layers unimportant.  They
mean only that their correspondence or preservation argument is not supplied
by the frozen Rocq theorem.

{1 Alignment Rule}

Alignment is semantic and directional:

{[
active OCaml object
  <-> mathematical role in POPL PaperCore
]}

It is not:

{[
Rocq file or proof stage
  -> mandatory OCaml module with the same record decomposition
]}

An OCaml abstraction is retained only when the implementation uses it or when
it expresses a genuine implementation invariant.  A dormant view is not an
adequacy proof and must not be preserved merely because it resembles a Rocq
record.

The current gaps and the direct object-by-object comparison are recorded in
{!page-rocq_ocaml_adequacy}.
